  "notes": "The major-label punk-to-arena producer who turned pop-punk into orchestrated rock theatre. 75–190 straight throughout — ballads at 74–105, punk at 143–190 — and because the research gives one backbeat class for the whole span, a single session window carries it with the mode on the punk end. Harmony is mixolydian ♭VII on the punk records, documented as I–IV–♭VII–IV verses with a IV–I–V–I chorus, so both are authored and mixolydian leads the scales. Three drum deltas are quoted: \"straight 8th kick with snare on 2&4 at punk tempo\" (a 4–8 density on all four anchors), \"**marching 16th snare figures for intros and interludes**\" (the snare roll pinned to 16ths, even grouping, almost no gaps), and \"timpani and orchestral bass drum for theatrical fills\", which is the tomLow/tom/crash perc block. His signature structural device is the **documented tempo architecture** — a track that starts at 75 BPM and moves to 194 felt in half-time — and since a session block carries one tempo, that is expressed as the very wide window plus the highest `switchUpProb` in this section and the multi-section structures. The palette expansion *is* the production: horns, Hammond organ, piano and synth added to punk records, plus gang-choir vocals as a chorus instrument, which is the sustained-pad counter. ⚠ The **INFERRED** parameters are every drum probability; the progressions, tempi, guitars, palette and arrangement architecture are sourced per record, the values are not.",
